# Frontend

- Start with semantic server-rendered HTML; HTMX and Alpine.js are progressive enhancements.
- Keep partial templates beside their feature and prefix names with `_`.
- HTMX endpoints must return intentional fragments and useful HTTP error states. Support direct/full-page access where practical.
- Set `hx-target`, `hx-swap`, loading indicators, focus behavior, and history behavior explicitly.
- Preserve form values and render field errors plus an accessible error summary after validation failures.
- Include CSRF protection on every unsafe request and guard against duplicate submissions.
- Use Alpine.js only for local ephemeral state such as disclosures, tabs, focus, and previews. Never duplicate permissions, validation, or durable workflow state in Alpine.
- Use Tailwind CSS and semantic tokens. Reuse components/utilities before adding arbitrary values or one-off colors.
- Follow the 4px spacing grid, Inter/system font stack, and light/dark/system themes.
- Meet WCAG 2.2 AA: labelled controls, semantic landmarks, visible focus, keyboard operation, adequate contrast, useful live/status messaging, and reduced-motion support.
- Aim for 44×44px interactive targets.
- Use a 56–64px top bar, approximately 240px wide-screen sidebar, compact-screen drawer, fluid gutters, and single-column compact forms.
- Make data tables intentionally responsive through column prioritization, cards, or labelled horizontal scrolling.
- Provide explicit loading, saving, empty, error, offline, conflict, and success states when relevant.
- Default user-facing product copy to clear Vietnamese; keep developer-facing identifiers in English.
- Do not add a frontend framework or client-side router.
